# Voice performance: Story (2008) vowel [ɑ] tract, fold card of the licensed two-mass pack, target note C4 (261.63 Hz).
# Every number below is cited. UNKNOWN marks a quantity no cited source gives; none is fitted to a recording.
#
# TRACT: Story, B. H. (2008). Comparison of magnetic resonance imaging-based vocal tract area functions obtained from the same speaker in 1994 and 2002. J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 123(1):327-335, doi:10.1121/1.2805683; author manuscript PMC2377017.
#   Table I column [ɑ], sections 1-44 glottis to lips, in file order. Areas are the printed
#   cm^2 digits scaled by e-4 (to m^2); the section length is the printed Delta (cm) scaled by e-2 (to m):
#   Delta = 0.388 cm, VTL = 44 Delta = 17.09 cm. One male speaker, MRI of 2002, vowel sustained supine.
#   LIMIT: a speech configuration, not a singing posture (larynx height, pharynx and lip shape differ in singing).
#   Same-specimen oracle for the passive tract (Table II, same speaker, same vowel):
#     measured  F1N 715±9  F2N 1180±31  F3N 2691±21 Hz; Fo 154 Hz
#     computed  F1C 694  F2C 942  F3C 2948 Hz (the paper's own model)
#
# FOLD: lower/upper mass 0.125 g / 0.025 g, k 80 / 8 N/m, coupling 25 N/m, fold length 1.4 cm, lower-mass
#   thickness 0.25 cm: Ishizaka & Flanagan (1972) two-mass set in the Steinecke & Herzel (1995) form, as carried by
#   the CC-BY model-card pack twomass-if72-standard-plos-pone0187486 (see crates/fs-couple/src/glottis.rs).
#   Rest half-gap 3.57e-4 m = Steinecke-Herzel rest area 0.05 cm^2 / 1.4 cm. Damping ratios 0.1 lower, 0.6 upper
#   open: Ishizaka & Flanagan (1972) p. 1238, their typical condition, and the law adds 1.0 to each while its own section
#   is shut (eq 3 and 4; per mass is the working reading, see glottis.rs). Collision law and mucosal delay are fixed in
#   glottis.rs. RETRIM on this law: only q moved, pressure held, emergent f0 to within 3 cents of the note, no recording, q 1.87995 to 3.69526.
#   Trace:
#     q 1.87995: f0 203.70 Hz (-433.3 c) -> q 3.00792: f0 241.66 Hz (-137.4 c) -> q 3.74184: f0 262.89 Hz (+8.4 c) ->
#     q 3.69526: f0 261.58 Hz (-0.3 c)
#   Any other trace in this header is from an earlier law and ended at an earlier q.
#   FLOW LAW: the kinetic (Bernoulli) drop plus a Poiseuille viscous drop 12 mu d l^2 U / a^3 per channel section (a = l h), d = 0.25 cm
#   (lower) and 0.05 cm (upper), mu from the ambient gas: Ishizaka & Flanagan (1972); the section drops are printed as P12 and P21 in
#   Assaneo & Trevisan, arXiv:1312.2950, which also gives the 0.05 cm. The 0.05 cm is the card default (an optional eleventh `fold`
#   field would override it). Without the viscous term the flow has a corner at closure and the radiated harmonics fall at only
#   6 dB/octave. Forces on the masses keep the Steinecke-Herzel form (a stated simplification). Added in glottis.rs e2dd8616d.
#   PITCH CONTROL (model-only objective): the three fold stiffnesses are the pack values x q = 3.69526. q was found by
#   rendering and measuring the EMERGENT f0 of the radiated pressure (autocorrelation over 0.5-1.5 s of a 1.5 s trial render, then
#   re-measured on the full 3 s render) and iterating q until it was
#   within 3 cents of the target note C4 = 261.63 Hz (equal temperament, A4 = 440 Hz). No recording enters. Trace on the
#   sibling description:
#     q 1.86912: f0 260.87 Hz (-5.0 c) -> q 1.87995: f0 261.62 Hz (-0.0 c)
#   That trace is the RE-TRIM for the viscous flow law (music_render af6cee4e7dfb): it starts from the q the Bernoulli-only law had
#   settled on, rendered under the new law, 1.5 s trials. The earlier, Bernoulli-only trim of the same card (the pack model was then
#   unchanged) was:
#     q 1.79492: f0 255.72 Hz (-39.5 c) -> q 1.87872: f0 262.38 Hz (+5.0 c) -> q 1.86912: f0 261.62 Hz (-0.1 c)
#   The first rule tried (set the isolated in-phase mode f = sqrt((k1+k2)/(m1+m2))/2pi to the note) phonated 550-815 cents
#   sharp: the pair oscillates nearer its second eigenmode (200.5 Hz at q = 1, pack values) than its in-phase mode (120.4 Hz).
#   Eigenmodes from det(K - w^2 M) = 0 with the pack masses, stiffnesses and coupling; tract loading then moves f0 by a
#   vowel-dependent amount, which is why q differs between vowels at the same note.
# SUBGLOTTAL PRESSURE: AUTHORED PLAYER INPUT, 1691.95 Pa: 900 Pa x the q 1.87995 of the previous trim, held when q moved on this law, so no longer 900 Pa x the q above: the drive rule of the in-repo vowel gate,
#   tests/vowel_gates.rs vw-002, which scales the drive with the stiffness. UNKNOWN for the recorded speaker: Story (2008) reports none; a measured source
#   would be Holmberg, Hillman & Perkell (1988) subglottal pressures. Q1 sweeps it (voice_q1_subglottal_pressure_metamorphic).
#   Ramp 0 -> P over 150 ms, hold, ramp to 0 over the last 150 ms. The 150 ms RISE is cited: Fant and Kruckenberg 2005,
#   Interspeech, doi:10.21437/Interspeech.2005-425, Sec. 1 on Fig. 1: one male speaker (a laryngologist) reading prose, true
#   subglottal pressure by sonde: 'the duration of the rise to an initial value of the order of 6-8 cm H2O is 120-180 ms'
#   at the onset of a breath group. 150 ms is the centre of that range. LIMITS: speech, one subject, not a singer's gesture,
#   no adduction trajectory. Only the rise time is cited; the plateau P is this card's own authored value, and their
#   3-5 and 6-8 cm H2O levels are NOT applied. The earlier 60 ms was authored. Steady state, and so the pitch trim, is unchanged.
# GAS: saturated air at 310.15 K and 101325 Pa. UNKNOWN as a source: Story (2008) does not state the speed of sound
#   its calculation used; the value is the declared physiological state of tract air, not fitted.
# TERMINATION: flanged (the mouth modelled as a piston in an infinite baffle, matching the receiver below).
# WALL (cited): the yielding-wall setting of Flanagan et al. as given by Birkholz, Haesner & Kuerbis, ICASSP 2022
#   (doi 10.1109/ICASSP43922.2022.9746611), eq. (2): per-unit-area wall impedance Z = (1600 + j w 1.5) g s^-1 cm^-2,
#   i.e. mechanical resistance B = 1.6e4 Pa s/m (1600 g s^-1 cm^-2 x 10) and surface mass M = 15 kg/m^2 (1.5 g cm^-2 x 10).
#   Stevens' setting (B = 1.0e4, M = 20) was tried as a probe and behaves the same. STIFFNESS is UNKNOWN in that source: the authors
#   neglect it because the stiffness term is very small at and above the first resonance. The pin needs K > 0, so K = 1.48e6 Pa/m,
#   a 50 Hz wall resonance (an assumption, M (2 pi 50)^2). Sensitivity, probed on /u/ at C4: K = 8.5e6 gives the same regularity
#   (R(T)/R(0) 0.992 vs 0.993). The source reports human first-formant bandwidths of 50-90 Hz and rigid tubes below 50 Hz up to 2 kHz.
#   The rigid-wall twin of this file is story2008-a-C4.performance.
# MICROPHONE: 0.30 m from the mouth at 45 degrees off axis (x = z = 0.30 sin 45 deg), the geometry of the paper's
#   recordings (AKG CK92 at 30 cm, 45 degrees off axis). Infinite baffle: no head diffraction. 16 rings x 64 points,
#   8 kHz resolved band (quadrature admission: 2 pi f cell / c <= pi/4).
# LEVEL: full_scale_pa 8.0 Pa (112 dB SPL peak at the PCM ceiling) is a recording-level choice, the same for
#   every description so vowel levels compare; the loudest render (a-D4) peaks at 4.89 Pa. Output is never normalized.
# MECHANICS: 96 kHz physical clock (2 per output sample), read through the bandlimited differentiating receiver.
frankensim-voice-performance-v1
audio 48000 144000 2
full_scale_pa 8.0
ambient moist_air 310.15 101325 1.0
fold two_mass 0.000125 2.5e-05 295.621 29.5621 92.3816 0.1 3.57e-4 1.4e-2 2.5e-3
fold_damping_upper 0.6
termination flanged
tract_wall 15 1.48e+06 1.6e4
tract_source story-2008-jasa-123-327-table-I-a
tract_licence published measurements transcribed with citation (Story 2008 Table I); redistribution terms unconfirmed, owner to decide
